Object.assign((window.COI_EXPLAIN = window.COI_EXPLAIN || {}), {
  "244": {
    "s": "Fifth Schedule governs Scheduled Areas; Sixth Schedule governs tribal areas of four north-eastern States.",
    "e": "This article sets up two separate systems for tribal regions. The Fifth Schedule applies to Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in most States, giving the Governor special powers and providing for Tribes Advisory Councils. The Sixth Schedule applies only to tribal areas in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram, where elected Autonomous District Councils make laws and run local affairs. The list of States has been revised over time, mainly through the North-Eastern Areas (Reorganisation) Act, 1971 and later amendments."
  },
  "244A": {
    "s": "Parliament may create an autonomous State within Assam for certain tribal areas.",
    "e": "Added by the 22nd Amendment (1969), this allows Parliament, by ordinary law, to carve out an autonomous State inside Assam from the tribal areas listed in Part I of the Sixth Schedule table, with its own legislature, council of ministers, or both. The law can specify which State List and Concurrent List subjects it controls and its financial arrangements. Such a law is not treated as a constitutional amendment under Article 368. It has been used once: Meghalaya was formed as an autonomous State within Assam in 1970 under the Assam Reorganisation (Meghalaya) Act, 1969, before becoming a full State in 1972. Demands to use it for other hill districts of Assam have continued."
  },
  "245": {
    "s": "Parliament legislates for all of India; State legislatures legislate for their own State.",
    "e": "Parliament can make laws for the whole of India or any part of it, while a State legislature can make laws only for its own State. Clause (2) says a law of Parliament is not invalid just because it operates outside India, for example laws covering Indian citizens or companies abroad. State laws, by contrast, need a real territorial connection (a nexus) with the State to be valid."
  },
  "246": {
    "s": "Divides law-making subjects into Union, State and Concurrent Lists in the Seventh Schedule.",
    "e": "This is the core of Indian federalism. Parliament alone can legislate on subjects in List I (the Union List, e.g. defence, foreign affairs, banking); State legislatures alone on List II (the State List, e.g. police, public health, agriculture); and both on List III (the Concurrent List, e.g. criminal law, education, forests). The opening words of each clause establish a hierarchy: if a subject genuinely overlaps, the Union List prevails over the State and Concurrent Lists, and the Concurrent List prevails over the State List. Clause (4) lets Parliament legislate on any subject, even State List matters, for areas not included in any State, such as Union territories. Courts apply the doctrine of pith and substance, looking at the true nature of a law, so incidental spill-over into another list does not make it invalid. Entries are read broadly and liberally, since they are fields of power rather than narrow words. Article 246A, added in 2016, carves out goods and services tax from this scheme."
  },
  "246A": {
    "s": "Gives both Parliament and State legislatures power to make laws on GST.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 101st Amendment (2016) to introduce the Goods and Services Tax, this article departs from the usual division of taxing powers. Both Parliament and every State legislature can simultaneously make laws on GST, which is why India has Central GST and State GST levied on the same transaction. It overrides Articles 246 and 254, so the ordinary rule that a Union law prevails over a conflicting State law does not apply in the usual way to GST. However, Parliament alone can legislate on GST for inter-State supplies (Integrated GST, see Article 269A). The Explanation says GST on petroleum crude, diesel, petrol, natural gas and aviation fuel starts only from a date the GST Council recommends; these remain outside GST for now. In Union of India v. Mohit Minerals (2022) the Supreme Court read this article as conferring simultaneous, equal law-making power on the Union and the States over GST."
  },
  "247": {
    "s": "Parliament may set up additional courts to administer Union laws.",
    "e": "Parliament may create extra courts to better administer laws it has made, or existing laws on Union List subjects. This allows specialised or additional courts for central legislation without depending on State-created courts."
  },
  "248": {
    "s": "Parliament holds residuary power over subjects not listed in State or Concurrent Lists.",
    "e": "Any subject not mentioned in the State List or Concurrent List falls exclusively to Parliament; this includes power to impose any tax not listed anywhere. Entry 97 of the Union List says the same thing. Giving residuary power to the Centre (rather than the States) reflects the Constitution's tilt towards a strong Union. Since the 101st Amendment (2016), this power is subject to Article 246A, so the residuary power cannot be used to override the shared GST arrangement. Courts generally first try to fit a law into an existing entry before treating it as residuary."
  },
  "249": {
    "s": "Rajya Sabha can authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List subject in the national interest.",
    "e": "Normally Parliament cannot make laws on State List subjects. But if the Rajya Sabha (Council of States) passes a resolution, supported by at least two-thirds of members present and voting, declaring that it is necessary in the national interest, Parliament may legislate on that State subject for the whole or part of India. The Rajya Sabha is used because it represents the States. The resolution lasts up to one year and can be renewed for a year at a time any number of times. A law made under it stops having effect six months after the resolution expires, except for things already done. The 101st Amendment (2016) extended this power to GST under Article 246A. States keep their own power to legislate on the subject, but conflicts are resolved in Parliament's favour under Article 251."
  },
  "250": {
    "s": "During a national emergency, Parliament can legislate on any State List subject.",
    "e": "While a Proclamation of Emergency under Article 352 is in force, Parliament may make laws on any State List matter, and since the 101st Amendment (2016) on State GST as well. In effect, the federal division of powers becomes temporarily unitary. Such laws lapse, to the extent Parliament otherwise lacked power, six months after the emergency ends, though anything done under them before then remains valid."
  },
  "251": {
    "s": "Parliament's laws under Articles 249 and 250 prevail over conflicting State laws.",
    "e": "States keep their power to legislate on the subject even when Parliament acts under Article 249 or 250. But if a State law conflicts with such a Union law, the Union law prevails, whether passed earlier or later. The State law is not void; it is merely inoperative to the extent of the conflict, and only while the Union law remains in force. Once the Union law lapses, the State law revives."
  },
  "252": {
    "s": "Two or more States can ask Parliament to legislate on a State subject for them.",
    "e": "If the legislatures of two or more States pass resolutions asking Parliament to regulate a State List matter, Parliament can pass a law that applies to those States. Other States can later adopt it by passing a similar resolution. Once applied, the law can be amended or repealed only by Parliament, not by the State legislature itself. Examples include the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972, the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 and the Urban Land (Ceiling and Regulation) Act, 1976."
  },
  "253": {
    "s": "Parliament can make any law needed to implement international treaties and agreements.",
    "e": "To implement any treaty, agreement or convention with other countries, or decisions of international bodies, Parliament can legislate for all of India even on State List subjects. This ensures that India can fulfil its international obligations. Environmental laws like the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 were enacted partly on this basis following the Stockholm Conference."
  },
  "254": {
    "s": "Resolves conflicts between Union and State laws; Union law generally prevails.",
    "e": "If a State law conflicts (is repugnant) with a valid law of Parliament, or with an existing law on a Concurrent List subject, the Union law prevails and the State law is void to the extent of the conflict. Clause (2) creates an important exception for Concurrent List subjects: if a State law conflicts with an earlier Union law but has been reserved for and received the President's assent, the State law prevails in that State. Even then, Parliament can later amend, vary or repeal the State law by a fresh law on the same matter. Courts treat repugnancy as arising where the two laws directly contradict each other, where Parliament intended to cover the whole field, or where both cannot be obeyed at the same time. Many State amendments to central laws on criminal procedure, labour and land acquisition have been made using the President's-assent route. The repugnancy rule is mainly applied to Concurrent List matters; where a Union List and State List entry clash, the issue is usually one of legislative competence under Article 246. Article 246A excludes GST from this rule."
  },
  "255": {
    "s": "Missing prior recommendation or sanction is cured if the proper authority later assents.",
    "e": "Some Bills require the prior recommendation or sanction of the President or Governor before introduction. This article treats that requirement as procedural only: if the relevant authority (or the President) later gives assent to the Act, the Act is not invalid merely because the earlier recommendation or sanction was missing. The reference to the Rajpramukh is a historical relic of former Part B States."
  },
  "256": {
    "s": "States must ensure compliance with Union laws; the Union may issue directions for this.",
    "e": "Each State must exercise its executive power in a way that ensures Union laws, and existing laws applicable in the State, are obeyed. The Union government can issue directions to a State as necessary for this purpose. This is a key tool of Union supervision in India's cooperative but Centre-leaning federal structure. If a State fails to comply with valid directions, Article 365 allows the President to treat it as a situation in which the State's government cannot be carried on per the Constitution, potentially leading to President's Rule under Article 356. In S.R. Bommai v. Union of India (1994), the Supreme Court stressed that such drastic consequences are subject to judicial review and must not be misused. In practice, directions under this article are rarely issued formally; disputes are usually resolved politically."
  },
  "257": {
    "s": "States must not obstruct Union executive power; Union may direct States on certain matters.",
    "e": "A State must exercise its executive power so as not to impede the Union's executive power, and the Union may give directions to ensure this. The Union can also direct States to build and maintain communication routes of national or military importance and to protect railways within the State. If following such directions costs the State more than it would normally spend, the Union must pay the extra cost, as agreed or as fixed by an arbitrator appointed by the Chief Justice of India."
  },
  "257A": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly allowed deployment of Union armed forces in States.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976), it allowed the Union to deploy its armed or other forces in any State to deal with grave law-and-order situations. It was removed by the 44th Amendment (1978, effective 1979); the subject is now covered by entry 2A of the Union List."
  },
  "258": {
    "s": "The Union may entrust its executive functions to State governments, or impose duties by law.",
    "e": "With a State's consent, the President can hand over Union executive functions to the State government or its officers, with or without conditions. Parliament can also, through a law applying in a State, confer powers and impose duties on State officers even in matters outside the State's legislative competence. When this happens, the Union must pay the State the extra administrative costs, as agreed or as decided by an arbitrator appointed by the Chief Justice of India."
  },
  "258A": {
    "s": "A State may entrust its executive functions to the Union, with the Union's consent.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 7th Amendment (1956), this is the reverse of Article 258. The Governor of a State may, with the Union government's consent, entrust any State executive function to the Union government or its officers, with or without conditions. It is used, for example, to let central agencies carry out projects that would normally be State responsibilities."
  },
  "259": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly dealt with armed forces in former Part B States.",
    "e": "This transitional article governed armed forces maintained by former princely-state unions (Part B States). It was removed by the 7th Amendment (1956) when the Part A/B/C classification of States was abolished."
  },
  "260": {
    "s": "India may take on governmental functions in foreign territories by agreement.",
    "e": "The Government of India may, by agreement with the government of a territory outside India, take on executive, legislative or judicial functions there. Every such arrangement must follow any law on foreign jurisdiction in force at the time. Historically, this was relevant to arrangements like the one with Sikkim before it became part of India."
  },
  "261": {
    "s": "Public acts, records and court judgments are recognised and enforceable throughout India.",
    "e": "The official acts, records and judicial proceedings of the Union and every State must be given full faith and credit across India. Parliament decides by law how they are proved and their effect (as done under the Evidence Act and its replacements). Final civil court judgments and orders from any part of India can be executed anywhere in India in accordance with law."
  },
  "262": {
    "s": "Parliament may provide for resolving inter-State river water disputes and exclude court jurisdiction.",
    "e": "Parliament can make laws for adjudicating disputes between States over the use, distribution or control of waters of inter-State rivers and river valleys. It may also bar the Supreme Court and other courts from hearing such disputes. Parliament has done so through the Inter-State River Water Disputes Act, 1956, which provides for special tribunals, and the River Boards Act, 1956. Tribunals have been set up for disputes such as the Cauvery, Krishna, Narmada, Godavari and Mahadayi. The Supreme Court has held that the bar does not prevent it from hearing appeals against a tribunal's final decision through Article 136, or from enforcing a tribunal's award, as it did in the Cauvery case in 2018. Delays in tribunal decisions and in implementing awards have been a recurring concern."
  },
  "263": {
    "s": "The President may establish an Inter-State Council to resolve disputes and coordinate policy.",
    "e": "If the President thinks it serves the public interest, an Inter-State Council can be created to advise on disputes between States, discuss common issues among States or between the Union and States, and recommend better policy coordination. The President decides its duties, organisation and procedure. Following a recommendation of the Sarkaria Commission on Centre-State relations, a permanent Inter-State Council was set up by Presidential order in 1990, chaired by the Prime Minister with Chief Ministers and several Union Ministers as members. It is an advisory body and its recommendations are not binding. It has met only occasionally, so its practical role has been limited. Other bodies under this article include the Central Council of Health and Family Welfare."
  },
  "264": {
    "s": "Defines Finance Commission for Part XII as one constituted under Article 280.",
    "e": "This is a short definitional clause for Part XII on finance. Wherever Part XII refers to the Finance Commission, it means the Commission constituted under Article 280. It was substituted in this form by the 7th Amendment (1956)."
  },
  "265": {
    "s": "No tax can be levied or collected without the authority of law.",
    "e": "This principle, no taxation without law, means the government cannot impose or collect any tax by mere executive order; there must be a law passed by a competent legislature. The law must itself be valid, meaning within the legislature's competence and consistent with fundamental rights and other constitutional limits. Collecting money as a tax without valid legal backing is unconstitutional, and the taxpayer can claim a refund. Courts have read this as covering both the levy (imposition) and the collection of tax. The rule also covers rates, which cannot be raised beyond what the law allows. It is a constitutional right but not a fundamental right, so relief is sought through ordinary writs under Article 226 rather than Article 32. Courts also distinguish taxes from fees, which require a rough link to services rendered."
  },
  "266": {
    "s": "Creates the Consolidated Funds and Public Accounts of India and of each State.",
    "e": "All revenues the Union government receives, all loans it raises and all loan repayments it receives go into one fund called the Consolidated Fund of India; States likewise have their own Consolidated Fund. Other public money, such as provident fund deposits, savings deposits and court deposits, goes into the Public Account, where the government acts more like a banker or trustee. Money can be withdrawn from a Consolidated Fund only in accordance with law and for constitutional purposes, which normally means through an Appropriation Act passed by the legislature. Withdrawals from the Public Account do not require such legislative appropriation. This framework is the basis of legislative control over public spending. The article is subject to Article 267 (Contingency Fund) and to the provisions assigning tax proceeds to the States."
  },
  "267": {
    "s": "Allows creation of Contingency Funds for unforeseen expenditure pending legislative approval.",
    "e": "Parliament and each State legislature can create a Contingency Fund placed at the disposal of the President or Governor. It is used to advance money for unforeseen expenses before the legislature formally approves them; the amount is later recouped from the Consolidated Fund once approved. The Contingency Fund of India was established by the Contingency Fund of India Act, 1950."
  },
  "268": {
    "s": "Certain stamp duties are levied by the Union but collected and kept by States.",
    "e": "Stamp duties mentioned in the Union List (for instance on bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes) are levied by the Union, but collected and retained by the States where they arise; in Union territories the Union collects them. These proceeds do not go into the Consolidated Fund of India. The 101st Amendment (2016) removed excise duties on medicinal and toilet preparations from this article, as they were subsumed in GST."
  },
  "268A": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly covered service tax shared between Union and States.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 88th Amendment (2003) to provide for service tax levied by the Union and collected and appropriated by the Union and States, it was never brought into force. It was omitted by the 101st Amendment (2016) when service tax was subsumed into GST."
  },
  "269": {
    "s": "Taxes on inter-State sale and consignment of goods are levied by Union, assigned to States.",
    "e": "Taxes on inter-State sale or purchase of goods (other than newspapers) and on inter-State consignment of goods are levied and collected by the Union but handed over to the States, following principles Parliament lays down. These proceeds do not form part of the Consolidated Fund of India. Since the 101st Amendment (2016), this is subject to Article 269A, so most such transactions are now covered by Integrated GST. Central Sales Tax remains for goods outside GST, such as petroleum products. Parliament may set principles to decide when a sale or consignment is inter-State."
  },
  "269A": {
    "s": "Inter-State GST (IGST) is levied and collected by the Union and shared with States.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 101st Amendment (2016), this governs Integrated GST on supplies in the course of inter-State trade and on imports, which are treated as inter-State supplies. The Union levies and collects it, and the proceeds are apportioned between the Union and the States as Parliament provides on the GST Council's recommendations. Amounts apportioned to States, or used to pay State GST through input tax credit, do not form part of the Consolidated Fund of India, and vice versa. Parliament may set rules on the place of supply and when a supply is inter-State. This system keeps GST destination-based, so the consuming State gets the revenue."
  },
  "270": {
    "s": "Central taxes are shared between Union and States via the Finance Commission's formula.",
    "e": "All taxes in the Union List (except those under Articles 268, 269 and 269A, surcharges under Article 271, and cesses for specific purposes) are collected by the Union and shared with the States. The 80th Amendment (2000) created this divisible pool of all central taxes, replacing an older system where only income tax was compulsorily shared and excise duties optionally shared. The 101st Amendment (2016) added clauses (1A) and (1B) so that Central GST and the Union's share of Integrated GST are also shared with States. The percentage going to States, and how it is divided among them, is prescribed by the President after considering the Finance Commission's recommendations. For example, the 15th Finance Commission recommended a 41 percent share for the States, and the 16th Finance Commission (2026 to 2031) kept it at 41 percent. Because cesses and surcharges are excluded from the divisible pool, States have objected to the growing reliance on them by the Union."
  },
  "271": {
    "s": "Parliament may impose surcharges on certain taxes exclusively for Union purposes.",
    "e": "Parliament can increase taxes referred to in Articles 269 and 270 by adding a surcharge, and the entire surcharge goes to the Union rather than being shared with States. Since the 101st Amendment (2016), no surcharge can be levied on GST. Surcharges on income tax are a common example."
  },
  "272": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly allowed optional sharing of Union excise duties with States.",
    "e": "This article provided that Union excise duties (other than on medicinal and toilet preparations) could be shared with States if Parliament so provided. It was removed by the 80th Amendment (2000), when all central taxes were placed in a common divisible pool under Article 270."
  },
  "273": {
    "s": "Grants to jute-producing States in place of export duty share on jute.",
    "e": "Assam, Bihar, Odisha and West Bengal were given annual grants from the Consolidated Fund of India instead of a share of export duty on jute and jute products. These grants were to continue only while the duty was levied or for ten years from 1950, whichever ended earlier, so the article is now of historical interest."
  },
  "274": {
    "s": "President's prior recommendation is needed for Bills affecting taxes in which States are interested.",
    "e": "Any Bill or amendment in Parliament that imposes or changes a tax in which States have an interest, changes the meaning of agricultural income for income-tax purposes, affects the principles of distributing money to States, or imposes a Union surcharge, can be introduced only on the President's recommendation. A tax in which States are interested means one whose proceeds are wholly or partly assigned to States, or which determines payments to States. This safeguard protects States' financial interests."
  },
  "275": {
    "s": "Parliament may provide grants-in-aid to States in need, including for tribal welfare.",
    "e": "Parliament can by law provide grants-in-aid from the Consolidated Fund of India to States that need financial help, and the amounts can differ between States. These are statutory grants, typically given on the Finance Commission's recommendations, such as revenue deficit grants. The first proviso requires grants to fund development schemes, approved by the Union, for welfare of Scheduled Tribes and to raise administration of Scheduled Areas to the level of the rest of the State. The second proviso gives Assam special grants for its tribal areas. Clause (1A), added by the 22nd Amendment (1969), applies similar arrangements to an autonomous State formed under Article 244A. Until Parliament legislates, the President may provide these grants by order, but only after considering the Finance Commission's recommendations. These grants differ from discretionary grants under Article 282."
  },
  "276": {
    "s": "States can tax professions, trades and employment, capped at Rs 2,500 per person yearly.",
    "e": "States and local bodies may levy taxes on professions, trades, callings and employment, commonly known as professional tax, and such a law is not invalid merely because it resembles a tax on income. The total payable by one person to a State or a local body cannot exceed Rs 2,500 a year, a limit raised from Rs 250 by the 60th Amendment (1988). This power does not limit Parliament's power to tax income from professions."
  },
  "277": {
    "s": "Taxes lawfully levied by States or local bodies before 1950 may continue.",
    "e": "Taxes, duties, cesses or fees that States or local bodies were lawfully levying just before the Constitution began can continue and be used for the same purposes, even if they now fall under the Union List. This continues until Parliament provides otherwise by law. It was a transitional measure to avoid disrupting local finances."
  },
  "278": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly allowed financial agreements with former Part B States.",
    "e": "This transitional article allowed the Union to enter financial agreements with former princely-state unions (Part B States). It was removed by the 7th Amendment (1956) when the Part A/B/C classification of States was abolished."
  },
  "279": {
    "s": "Defines net proceeds of taxes and requires CAG certification of them.",
    "e": "Net proceeds means the tax collected minus the cost of collection. The Comptroller and Auditor-General of India certifies the net proceeds of any tax for any area, and that certificate is final. Parliament or the President may lay down how proceeds assigned to States are calculated, when payments are made and how adjustments between years are handled."
  },
  "279A": {
    "s": "Creates the GST Council of Union and State finance ministers to recommend GST policy.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 101st Amendment (2016), this establishes the Goods and Services Tax Council, chaired by the Union Finance Minister, with the Union Minister of State for Revenue or Finance and a minister nominated by each State. It recommends which taxes are subsumed in GST, which goods and services are taxed or exempt, model GST laws, place-of-supply rules, turnover thresholds, tax rates, special rates during disasters, special provisions for certain north-eastern and hill States, and when GST should apply to petroleum products. Decisions require a three-fourths majority of weighted votes of members present and voting: the Union's vote counts for one-third and all States together for two-thirds. This means neither the Union alone nor the States alone can push through a decision. The quorum is half the members, and decisions are not invalid due to vacancies or minor procedural defects. Clause (11) lets the Council set up a mechanism to resolve disputes between the Union and States or among States. In Union of India v. Mohit Minerals (2022), the Supreme Court held that the Council's recommendations are not binding on Parliament and State legislatures; they have persuasive value as the product of collaborative deliberation, and the Council functions as a forum for cooperative federalism. The Council has been the main forum for GST rate and policy changes since 2017."
  },
  "280": {
    "s": "The President constitutes a Finance Commission every five years to recommend sharing of revenues.",
    "e": "Every five years (or earlier if needed), the President appoints a Finance Commission with a Chairman and four other members; their qualifications are set by Parliament through the Finance Commission (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act, 1951. The Commission recommends how the net proceeds of shareable taxes should be divided between the Union and the States (vertical distribution), and how the States' share should be divided among them (horizontal distribution). It also recommends the principles for grants-in-aid to States from the Consolidated Fund of India. After the 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992), it also suggests measures to boost State funds for Panchayats and Municipalities based on State Finance Commission reports. The President may refer other matters in the interest of sound finance. Its recommendations are advisory, but are almost always accepted for tax devolution. The Fifteenth Finance Commission covered 2021 to 2026. The Sixteenth, constituted in December 2023 and chaired by Arvind Panagariya, covers 2026 to 2031; its report, tabled in Parliament in February 2026 and accepted on tax devolution, kept the States' share of the divisible pool at 41 percent."
  },
  "281": {
    "s": "Finance Commission recommendations must be laid before Parliament with an action-taken memorandum.",
    "e": "The President must place every Finance Commission recommendation before both Houses of Parliament, along with an explanatory memorandum on the action taken. This ensures parliamentary scrutiny and transparency about how the government has responded to the Commission's advice."
  },
  "282": {
    "s": "The Union or a State may make grants for any public purpose, even outside its legislative powers.",
    "e": "The Union and the States can make grants for any public purpose, even on subjects outside their law-making powers. This allows the Union to fund schemes in areas like health, education or agriculture that fall in the State List. It is the main constitutional basis for centrally sponsored schemes and other discretionary transfers that are not routed through the Finance Commission. Such grants were earlier often channelled on the advice of the Planning Commission, replaced by NITI Aayog in 2015. In Bhim Singh v. Union of India (2010), the Supreme Court upheld the MPLAD scheme, relying on this article and holding that the purpose must be a public purpose. States have criticised heavy use of this article as weakening their fiscal autonomy, since conditions are often attached to the grants."
  },
  "283": {
    "s": "Custody and withdrawal of public funds are regulated by law or by rules.",
    "e": "Parliament regulates by law the custody of the Consolidated Fund, Contingency Fund and Public Account of India, and payments into and withdrawals from them; until it does so, the President makes rules. State legislatures and Governors have the same powers over State funds. In practice these matters are largely governed by rules made by the President and Governors."
  },
  "284": {
    "s": "Money deposited with public officers or courts goes into the Public Account.",
    "e": "Money received by government officers in their official capacity (other than government revenues) and money deposited in courts for any case, account or person must be paid into the Public Account of India or of the State. This keeps such trust money under proper public accounting rather than in private hands."
  },
  "285": {
    "s": "Union property is exempt from State taxes unless Parliament provides otherwise.",
    "e": "States and local authorities cannot tax property belonging to the Union unless Parliament allows it by law. However, taxes that applied to Union property before 1950 may continue until Parliament decides otherwise. Municipalities therefore often receive service charges instead of property tax on central government buildings. The exemption covers the Union's own property, not that of separate corporations owned by it."
  },
  "286": {
    "s": "States cannot tax supplies outside the State or in the course of imports and exports.",
    "e": "A State cannot tax a supply of goods or services that takes place outside the State, or in the course of import into or export out of India. Parliament can lay down principles for deciding when a supply takes place in these ways. Originally about sales tax on goods, it was rewritten by the 101st Amendment (2016) to cover supplies of goods and services under GST. It prevents States from taxing transactions that belong to another State or to international trade."
  },
  "287": {
    "s": "States cannot tax electricity consumed by or sold to the Union or railways.",
    "e": "Unless Parliament provides otherwise, a State cannot tax electricity consumed by the Union government, or used for building, maintaining or operating railways by the Union or a railway company, or sold to them for such use. Any State tax on sale of electricity must ensure that the price charged to these buyers is lower by the amount of the tax."
  },
  "288": {
    "s": "Limits State taxes on water or electricity of inter-State river-valley authorities.",
    "e": "State laws in force in 1950 cannot tax water or electricity stored, generated, used or sold by authorities set up by law to regulate or develop inter-State rivers, such as the Damodar Valley Corporation, unless the President orders otherwise. A State may pass a new law imposing such a tax, but it takes effect only after it has been reserved for and received the President's assent."
  },
  "289": {
    "s": "State property and income are exempt from Union taxes, except for State trading or business.",
    "e": "The Union cannot tax the property and income of a State. However, Parliament may tax a State's trade or business, and related property and income, to the extent it provides by law. Parliament can also declare certain kinds of trade or business incidental to ordinary government functions, keeping them exempt. The exemption does not extend to corporations set up by States, which are treated as separate legal entities."
  },
  "290": {
    "s": "Adjusts court, commission and pension costs between the Union and States.",
    "e": "Where the costs of a court or commission, or pensions of government servants, are charged on one government's Consolidated Fund but the court, commission or person served another government, the costs are shared. The contribution is decided by agreement or, failing that, by an arbitrator appointed by the Chief Justice of India."
  },
  "290A": {
    "s": "Kerala and Tamil Nadu must make fixed annual payments to Devaswom funds for temples.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 7th Amendment (1956), this requires Kerala to pay Rs 46.5 lakh a year to the Travancore Devaswom Fund, and Tamil Nadu to pay Rs 13.5 lakh a year to a Devaswom Fund for Hindu temples in areas transferred to it from Travancore-Cochin in 1956. It honours commitments made when the princely state of Travancore merged with India. These payments are charged on the State's Consolidated Fund, so they do not need an annual vote."
  },
  "291": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly guaranteed privy purses to former princely rulers.",
    "e": "This article guaranteed tax-free annual privy purse payments to former rulers of princely states under their accession agreements. It was omitted by the 26th Amendment (1971), which abolished privy purses and the recognition of rulers."
  },
  "292": {
    "s": "The Union may borrow and give guarantees on the security of the Consolidated Fund.",
    "e": "The Union government can borrow money on the security of the Consolidated Fund of India and give guarantees, within limits Parliament may set by law. Parliament has never fixed a firm statutory ceiling, but the Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003 sets deficit and debt targets."
  },
  "293": {
    "s": "States may borrow within India, but need Union consent while Union loans are outstanding.",
    "e": "A State can borrow only within India, on the security of its Consolidated Fund and within limits its legislature may set. The Union can lend to States or guarantee their loans. Importantly, a State with any outstanding loan from the Union, or a Union-guaranteed loan, must get the Union's consent before raising a new loan, and the Union may attach conditions. Since nearly all States owe money to the Union, this gives the Union significant control over State borrowing. In April 2024 the Supreme Court referred questions about the scope of this power, raised in a suit by Kerala, to a five-judge Constitution Bench; no ruling had been reported as of late 2026."
  },
  "294": {
    "s": "Union and States inherit the property, rights and liabilities of pre-1950 governments.",
    "e": "From 26 January 1950, property and assets held by the British Crown for the Dominion of India and for each Governor's Province passed to the Union and the corresponding State. Their rights, liabilities and obligations, including contracts, also passed to the new governments, subject to adjustments arising from the partition and creation of Pakistan."
  },
  "295": {
    "s": "Property and liabilities of former princely states pass to the Union for Union purposes.",
    "e": "Property, assets, rights and liabilities of former princely states that became Part B States vested in the Union, where they related to Union List purposes, subject to agreements with those States. The remainder passed to the corresponding State. This was a transitional provision for integrating princely states."
  },
  "296": {
    "s": "Ownerless property, or property by escheat or lapse, goes to the State or Union.",
    "e": "Property that would earlier have gone to the British Crown or a princely Ruler because there was no rightful owner or heir (escheat, lapse or bona vacantia) now vests in the State where it is located, or otherwise in the Union. If such property was already in a government's possession, it goes to whichever government (Union or State) was using it."
  },
  "297": {
    "s": "Seabed resources in territorial waters, continental shelf and EEZ belong to the Union.",
    "e": "All land, minerals and valuables under the sea within India's territorial waters, continental shelf and exclusive economic zone belong to the Union, as do all other resources of the exclusive economic zone. Parliament fixes the limits of these maritime zones by law, as done in the Territorial Waters, Continental Shelf, Exclusive Economic Zone and Other Maritime Zones Act, 1976. The article in its current form was substituted by the 40th Amendment (1976). Coastal States therefore do not own offshore resources."
  },
  "298": {
    "s": "Union and States may carry on trade, own property and make contracts.",
    "e": "Both the Union and State governments can run businesses, buy, hold and sell property, and enter contracts for any purpose. If the Union runs a business on a subject outside Parliament's law-making power, it is subject to State laws, and vice versa. Substituted in its present form by the 7th Amendment (1956), it is the basis for government enterprises and public procurement, though such actions remain subject to fundamental rights like Article 14."
  },
  "299": {
    "s": "Government contracts must be made in the name of the President or Governor.",
    "e": "Contracts made by the Union or a State must be expressed as made by the President or Governor, and executed by persons authorised by them in the prescribed manner. Courts have treated these requirements as mandatory: a contract not made in this form is not binding on the government, though a party may still claim compensation for benefits received under Section 70 of the Contract Act. The President, the Governor and the officers who sign are not personally liable on such contracts."
  },
  "300": {
    "s": "The Union and States can sue and be sued in their own names.",
    "e": "The Union can sue or be sued as the Union of India, and a State in its own name. Their liability is the same as that of the Dominion of India and the Provinces before 1950, unless a law provides otherwise. Courts have used this to hold the government liable for wrongful acts of its employees, while recognising some immunity for sovereign functions, a distinction that has narrowed over time. Pending cases involving the Dominion, Provinces or princely states continued with the new governments substituted."
  },
  "300A": {
    "s": "No one can be deprived of property except by authority of law.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 44th Amendment (1978), this article replaced the fundamental right to property under Articles 19(1)(f) and 31, which were deleted. The right to property is now a constitutional right, not a fundamental right, so a person cannot directly approach the Supreme Court under Article 32 for its violation, but can seek relief from a High Court under Article 226. Deprivation must be backed by a valid law; mere executive action is not enough. In K.T. Plantation v. State of Karnataka (2011), the Supreme Court held that a law taking property must serve a public purpose, and that although this article does not expressly mention compensation, a right to compensation can be inferred from it, subject to judicial review. In Vidya Devi v. State of Himachal Pradesh (2020), the Court described the right to property as a human right and held that the State cannot take land without following due process. In Kolkata Municipal Corporation v. Bimal Kumar Shah (2024), the Court said that authority of law includes seven procedural sub-rights, such as notice, hearing, a reasoned decision, acquisition only for a public purpose and fair compensation. Acquisition laws, such as the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013, now provide for compensation."
  },
  "301": {
    "s": "Trade, commerce and movement throughout India shall be free.",
    "e": "This article guarantees that trade, commerce and intercourse across India are free, aiming to make the country a single economic market without internal barriers. It protects movement of goods, services and people both between States and within a State. The freedom is subject to the other provisions of Part XIII, which allow reasonable restrictions by Parliament and States (Articles 302 to 305). Courts have held that it is aimed at restrictions that directly and immediately impede the flow of trade, not merely regulatory measures that facilitate trade. In Atiabari Tea Co. v. State of Assam (1961) the Supreme Court held that taxes can violate it, and in Automobile Transport (Rajasthan) v. State of Rajasthan (1962) it added that regulatory or compensatory taxes do not. In Jindal Stainless Ltd. v. State of Haryana (2016), a nine-judge bench rejected the compensatory-tax test and held that non-discriminatory taxes generally do not violate this article; only discriminatory taxes are hit. It is not a fundamental right, but a citizen can challenge laws that violate it."
  },
  "302": {
    "s": "Parliament may restrict freedom of trade and commerce in the public interest.",
    "e": "Parliament can make laws restricting trade, commerce or movement between States or within any part of India, if required in the public interest. This is the main exception to Article 301. Examples include laws controlling essential commodities or regulating movement of goods during shortages."
  },
  "303": {
    "s": "Neither Parliament nor States may favour or discriminate between States in trade laws.",
    "e": "Parliament and State legislatures cannot make trade and commerce laws that give preference to one State over another or discriminate between States. The exception is that Parliament may do so if it declares it necessary to deal with scarcity of goods in any part of India. This prevents the Union from using its trade powers to play favourites among States."
  },
  "304": {
    "s": "States may tax imported goods without discrimination and impose reasonable trade restrictions.",
    "e": "Despite Articles 301 and 303, a State may tax goods brought from other States or Union territories, provided similar goods produced locally are taxed the same way, so there is no discrimination. A State may also impose reasonable restrictions on trade with or within the State in the public interest, but such a Bill needs the President's prior sanction before introduction. If the President later assents, Article 255 cures the absence of prior sanction."
  },
  "305": {
    "s": "Saves existing laws and laws creating State monopolies from the freedom-of-trade rules.",
    "e": "Articles 301 and 303 do not affect laws existing in 1950 unless the President directs otherwise. Also, Article 301 does not prevent laws creating State monopolies in a trade, business, industry or service, as allowed by Article 19(6)(ii). This was substituted by the 4th Amendment (1955) to protect nationalisation and government monopolies."
  },
  "306": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly let certain Part B States restrict trade and commerce.",
    "e": "This transitional article allowed certain former princely-state unions (Part B States) to continue some trade restrictions and taxes for a limited period. It was removed by the 7th Amendment (1956) when the Part A/B/C classification of States was abolished."
  },
  "307": {
    "s": "Parliament may appoint an authority to carry out the freedom-of-trade provisions.",
    "e": "Parliament may set up a suitable authority, with powers and duties it decides, to carry out the purposes of Articles 301 to 304. No such dedicated authority has been created so far, and disputes are handled by the courts."
  },
  "308": {
    "s": "Defines State for Part XIV; textually excludes Jammu and Kashmir.",
    "e": "For Part XIV on government services, State did not include Jammu and Kashmir, which had its own constitution and service rules. After Article 370 was effectively abrogated and the State was reorganised into Union territories in 2019, Jammu and Kashmir is no longer a State, so this exclusion has little practical effect, though the words remain in the official text."
  },
  "309": {
    "s": "Legislatures may regulate recruitment and service conditions; executive rules apply meanwhile.",
    "e": "Parliament and State legislatures can make laws on recruitment and conditions of service of government employees. Until they do, the President (for Union services) and Governor (for State services) can make rules, which are subject to any such law. Most service matters, such as pay, promotion and discipline, are in fact governed by these rules. The rules can be retrospective but must not violate fundamental rights such as equality under Articles 14 and 16."
  },
  "310": {
    "s": "Government employees hold office during the pleasure of the President or Governor.",
    "e": "Members of defence services, civil services and all-India services hold office at the pleasure of the President, or of the Governor for State services, a doctrine inherited from English law. This means their service can in principle be ended at will, but the pleasure doctrine is limited by Article 311 and other constitutional provisions. Courts have held that it must be exercised fairly and not arbitrarily. Contracts with specially qualified persons may provide for compensation if their post is abolished or they are removed early without misconduct."
  },
  "311": {
    "s": "Protects civil servants against dismissal without a fair inquiry or by a junior authority.",
    "e": "This article gives civil servants two key safeguards. First, a civil servant cannot be dismissed or removed by an authority lower in rank than the one who appointed them. Second, no one can be dismissed, removed or reduced in rank without an inquiry in which they are told the charges and given a reasonable opportunity to be heard. After the 42nd Amendment (1976), there is no second hearing on the proposed penalty; it can be imposed on the evidence from the inquiry. The inquiry can be dispensed with if the person has been convicted on a criminal charge, if the authority records in writing that holding an inquiry is not reasonably practicable, or if the President or Governor is satisfied that it is not expedient in the interest of the security of the State. In Union of India v. Tulsiram Patel (1985), the Supreme Court upheld these exceptions but said the reasons must be relevant and are open to limited judicial review. The protections apply only to punitive dismissals, removals or reductions in rank, not to termination of a temporary or probationary employee in accordance with their terms of service without any stigma."
  },
  "312": {
    "s": "Parliament may create all-India services common to the Union and States, on Rajya Sabha's resolution.",
    "e": "If the Rajya Sabha passes a resolution by a two-thirds majority of members present and voting that it is in the national interest, Parliament may create one or more all-India services common to the Union and States. These officers are recruited centrally but serve both the Union and the States. The Indian Administrative Service and Indian Police Service are treated as created under this article, and the Indian Forest Service was created in 1966. The 42nd Amendment (1976) added the possibility of an all-India judicial service, limited to posts of district judge and above. Such a law can amend provisions about subordinate courts without being treated as a constitutional amendment. An all-India judicial service has been proposed several times but has not yet been created. The All India Services Act, 1951 governs recruitment and conditions of service of these officers."
  },
  "312A": {
    "s": "Parliament may vary or revoke service conditions of former Secretary of State officers.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 28th Amendment (1972), this allowed Parliament to change, even retrospectively, the pay, leave, pension and disciplinary rights of officers appointed by the British Secretary of State before 1950, mainly the Indian Civil Service. It ended the special protections they enjoyed, putting them on par with other civil servants. With these officers long retired, it is now of historical interest."
  },
  "313": {
    "s": "Pre-1950 service laws continue until replaced, if consistent with the Constitution.",
    "e": "Laws applicable to public services and posts in force just before the Constitution began continue to apply to those services, as long as they are consistent with the Constitution, until other provision is made. This ensured continuity in government administration."
  },
  "314": {
    "s": "Omitted — formerly protected existing officers of certain services like the ICS.",
    "e": "This article protected the pre-1950 conditions of service of officers appointed by the British Secretary of State, such as Indian Civil Service officers. It was omitted by the 28th Amendment (1972), which also inserted Article 312A."
  },
  "315": {
    "s": "Establishes Public Service Commissions for the Union and each State.",
    "e": "There must be a Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) and a Public Service Commission for each State. Two or more States can agree, by resolutions of their legislatures, to ask Parliament to create a Joint State Public Service Commission. The UPSC can, at a Governor's request and with the President's approval, serve the needs of a State."
  },
  "316": {
    "s": "Governs appointment, term and tenure of Public Service Commission members.",
    "e": "The President appoints the Chairman and members of the UPSC and Joint Commissions; the Governor appoints those of State Commissions. About half the members must have at least ten years of government service. Members hold office for six years or until age 65 (UPSC) or 62 (State and Joint Commissions), whichever is earlier; the State age limit was raised from 60 by the 41st Amendment (1976). They cannot be reappointed to the same office, and they may resign or be removed under Article 317. If the Chairman's post is vacant, an acting Chairman may be appointed from among the members."
  },
  "317": {
    "s": "PSC members can be removed for misbehaviour only after a Supreme Court inquiry.",
    "e": "The Chairman or a member of a Public Service Commission can be removed for misbehaviour only by the President, after the Supreme Court, on a reference by the President, inquires and recommends removal. They can be suspended while the reference is pending. The President can also remove them for insolvency, taking paid employment outside their duties, or infirmity of mind or body. Having an interest in a government contract, or sharing its profits other than as a member of a company, counts as misbehaviour. This protects the independence of the Commissions."
  },
  "318": {
    "s": "President or Governor regulates the strength and service conditions of PSC members and staff.",
    "e": "The President (for the UPSC and Joint Commissions) and the Governor (for State Commissions) decide, through regulations, the number of members and staff and their service conditions. A member's service conditions cannot be changed to their disadvantage after appointment, which safeguards their independence."
  },
  "319": {
    "s": "Restricts further government employment of PSC members after their term ends.",
    "e": "To ensure independence, the UPSC Chairman cannot take any further government employment after leaving office. A State PSC Chairman can only become Chairman or member of the UPSC or Chairman of another State PSC. A UPSC member can only become Chairman of the UPSC or a State PSC. A State PSC member can become Chairman or member of the UPSC or Chairman of a State PSC. None of them may take other government jobs."
  },
  "320": {
    "s": "Sets out the functions of Public Service Commissions, including exams and consultation.",
    "e": "The UPSC and State PSCs conduct examinations for government recruitment, and the UPSC helps States with joint recruitment schemes on request. They must be consulted on methods of recruitment, principles for appointments, promotions and transfers, disciplinary matters, legal costs claims and injury pensions. Regulations can exclude certain matters from consultation, and consultation is not needed for reservations under Article 16(4) or 335. In State of U.P. v. Manbodhan Lal Srivastava (1957), the Supreme Court held that the consultation requirement is directory, not mandatory, so failure to consult does not by itself invalidate the government's action, and the Commissions' advice is not binding."
  },
  "321": {
    "s": "Legislatures may give Public Service Commissions additional functions by law.",
    "e": "Parliament or a State legislature can, by law, give the UPSC or a State PSC additional functions, including for services of local authorities, statutory corporations or public institutions. This lets the Commissions' merit-based recruitment extend beyond core government jobs."
  },
  "322": {
    "s": "PSC expenses are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India or the State.",
    "e": "The expenses of the UPSC and State PSCs, including salaries, allowances and pensions of members and staff, are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India or the State. Charged expenditure is not voted on by the legislature, which protects the Commissions' financial independence."
  },
  "323": {
    "s": "Public Service Commissions must submit annual reports to be laid before the legislature.",
    "e": "The UPSC reports annually to the President, and State and Joint Commissions to the relevant Governor. These reports, with a memorandum explaining why any advice of the Commission was not accepted, must be laid before Parliament or the State legislature. This promotes accountability where the government departs from the Commission's advice."
  },
  "323A": {
    "s": "Parliament may set up administrative tribunals for disputes concerning public service employees.",
    "e": "Inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976), this allows Parliament to create administrative tribunals to decide disputes about recruitment and conditions of service of government employees, including those of local bodies and government-owned corporations. There can be a Central tribunal and separate tribunals for one or more States. The law can set their jurisdiction, powers (including contempt), procedure, transfer pending cases to them, and exclude the jurisdiction of all courts except the Supreme Court under Article 136. Parliament enacted the Administrative Tribunals Act, 1985, creating the Central Administrative Tribunal and State tribunals. In L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997), a seven-judge bench held that judicial review by the High Courts under Articles 226 and 227 and the Supreme Court under Article 32 is part of the basic structure, so the clause excluding courts' jurisdiction was struck down to that extent. As a result, tribunal decisions can be challenged before a High Court division bench before going to the Supreme Court. Tribunals thus act as courts of first instance and supplement, rather than replace, the High Courts."
  },
  "323B": {
    "s": "Legislatures may set up tribunals for taxes, labour, land reform, elections, rent and more.",
    "e": "Also inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976), this allows Parliament or a State legislature, within its law-making powers, to create tribunals for matters such as taxation, foreign exchange and customs, industrial and labour disputes, land reforms, urban land ceilings, certain election disputes, essential goods and prices, and rent and tenancy (added by the 75th Amendment, 1993). Such laws may set up a hierarchy of tribunals and exclude other courts' jurisdiction except the Supreme Court's under Article 136. However, after L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997), the High Courts' and Supreme Court's writ jurisdiction cannot be excluded. Many tribunals today are also created under other legislative powers, and the Supreme Court has repeatedly scrutinised the independence of tribunals, including in the Madras Bar Association line of cases on tribunal appointments and tenure."
  }
});
